lesson physics-the-shape-of-a-sound v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=2 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The shape of a sound"
  goal "Read a sound's pitch and loudness from the shape of its wave, and work out its vibrations a second in hertz."

  look {
    scene 36x13 {
      row all at=canvas(0, 0) space=1 align=end {
        wave s waves=[3, 6] heights=[3, 1] across=10 letters=1 blank=-1
        whale w spout=1 facing=-1
      }
    }
    say """
      A sound is something vibrating. A microphone turns the vibrations into a wave on a screen, and
      every screen here shows 0.01 s. The more waves across the screen, the faster the vibrations and
      the higher the note: A has 3 waves, so it vibrates 300 times a second, 300 hertz (Hz), and B is
      600 Hz. The taller the waves, the harder it vibrates and the louder it is, so A is louder.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.wave-hertz count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    level easy {
      worked physics.wave-higher v=0 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.wave-higher count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.wave-loudest count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.wave-what-changed count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.wave-band count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.wave-half-band count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.hear-range count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
  }
  story {
    show physics.wave-whale w=2 s=8 easy-w=1 easy-s=6 hard-w=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.wave-harder-pluck w=3 hard-w=5
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.wave-length-for v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "More waves across the screen is a higher note; taller waves are a louder one."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The usual mix-up is between high and loud: children call a loud sound "high" and a quiet one
    "low", and pick the taller wave as the higher note. The questions keep changing both, so the
    child has to count waves for pitch and look at height for loudness. Listen for the child
    counting whole waves, from one crossing of the middle line to the next going the same way. If
    the hertz stall, go back to one screen of 2 waves: 0.01 s, so a hundred screens a second, so 200
    Hz. Off the page, hum low and high with a hand on your throat, or pluck a ruler over the edge of
    a table and shorten the part that shakes. The three-star question is solved by noticing that
    length times hertz stays the same for one band.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the screens have few waves, a worked example picks the higher note, the traces keep one thing the same, only people and dogs are compared by hearing, and the three-star band doubles its note."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the screens are crowded, the taller trace is often the lower note, the harder pluck and the whale have more waves, a bat is among the sounds, and the three-star band goes from 300 to 400 Hz."
  }
}
